{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And if the plague returns.</b> Rashi: 'Could it mean — if it withdrew, it is impure? It writes ושב הכהן etc. until ובא הכהן וראה והנה פשה — could it mean that it is not impure unless it spreads? It says צרעת ממארת בבתים etc. — just as there, with garments, the returning one is impure even though it does not spread, so too here the returning one is impure even though it does not spread etc.' The proper place of this verse is not here but rather after 'ונתץ את הבית' — it should have been written after ואם ישוב. That is, this is not its primary place; rather, immediately after והאוכל בבית, as Rashi explains below. And one should not fix it between the two occurrences of שוב, since the entire matter is contiguous and one should not interrupt in between. Also one should not fix it after ואם בא יבא, because the placing of ולקח לחטאת את הבית adjacent to כי נרפא הנגע is required, until its conclusion. And along with the drag of נתץ it was drawn here, because there is one who arrives at the time when he fulfills after it 'ונתץ את הבית,' as explained below. וראה הכהן והנה פשה — 'the הא does not come to teach etc.' We learn the 'coming' mentioned here — from the 'returning' — which is stated regarding it ושב הכהן: just as the 'returning' on the seventh day for quarantine is as written, ושב הכהן ביום השביעי וראה והנה פשה הנגע — [what is the 'returning'?] he dismantles, as written, וחלצו את האבנים; and scrapes, as written, ואת הבית יקציע; and plasters, as written, ואם ישוב [את] הנגע ונתץ את הבית — and he gives it a week, to see whether the plague returns in it, so that it would be demolished, as written, ואם ישוב הנגע etc. ונתץ את הבית. So too this 'coming' of ובא הכהן mentioned here — which speaks of one who stood in quarantine the first week and spread in the quarantine of the second week — he dismantles and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week. How so? He stood in quarantine the first week, and he again quarantined it a second week, and it spread, and the priest came at the end of the quarantine of the second week and saw that it spread in the second week — he dismantles and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week, to see whether the plague returns in it during this week — ונתץ את הבית. If it did not return, it is pure. And from where do we know about one who stood in both — meaning, it did not spread in either of the two quarantines, but remained at its original size when the priest first came to inspect it — from where do we know that he dismantles and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week? It writes ואם בא יבא. About whom is it speaking? If about one that spread in the quarantine of the first week — its law is already stated at 'ושב הכהן.' And if about one that spread in the quarantine of the second week — its law is already stated at 'ובא הכהן.' Thus it is not saying ואם בא יבא הכהן except about both one who comes at the end of the quarantine of the first week and one who comes at the end of the quarantine of the second week, וראה והנה לא פשה — this is 'stood in both.' This one that remained — what is done to it? Could one say he releases it and it goes? As written here, וטהר הכהן את הבית — it writes כי נרפא הנגע: I did not declare pure except the healing — the plague appeared to have healed; let us see what is done to it. I derive it from two 'comings': the 'coming' stated above — ובא הכהן — and the 'coming' stated below — ואם בא יבא הכהן. Just as the 'coming' stated above dismantles and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week, as we derived above, learning 'coming' from 'returning' — so too the 'coming' stated below dismantles and scrapes and plasters and gives it a week etc. And if the priest comes at the end of the quarantine of the second week and sees that it has not spread — וטהר הכהן את הבית כי נרפא הנגע — meaning: if it healed by means of dismantling and scraping and plastering and the giving of a third week, and the plague did not return; but if it returned, it is demolished. Conclusion of the matter etc.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "This comment reproduces Rashi's commentary on Leviticus 14:43–48. The bracketed editorial note at the beginning is Chizkuni's own, indicating he copied Rashi's interpretation for the reader's benefit. The text is dense with abbreviations, dashes, and compressed midrashic argumentation; the dash-separated phrases (e.g., 'בהסגר שבוע - ראשון') represent Rashi's step-by-step exclusions and are rendered with em-dashes preserved."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:28.515956901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}